{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And Elohim created the great sea-monsters.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): they are fish, and because of the extraordinary size of their bodies Scripture attributed their creation to Elohim — just as you find in the case of man, on account of his rank, that Scripture attributed his creation to Elohim, as it is written, ״ויברא אלהים את האדם בצלמו״. And the intention is that the Creator, blessed be He, brought those large bodies into being from nothingness. And the philosophers wrote in their books that they knew of some that were five hundred parasangs long. Also our masters, the Sages of the Talmud, recounted to us extraordinary measures in tractate Bava Batra — this is what Rabbah bar bar Ḥannah testified and said: that fish which rose and died — carrion entered its mouth, and it died, and the sea cast it on shore, and from it sixty towns were cut, and sixty towns were consumed from it, and sixty towns were salted from it, and from one of its eyeballs they made three hundred flasks of oil; the next year, when we returned after twelve months of the year, we saw them sawing beams from its bones to rebuild those towns. And that same sage further testified on his own account: we were going in a ship, and we saw that fish with a growth of grass on its back; we thought it was dry land, so we went up and baked and cooked on it; when it grew hot it turned over, and had we not been near the ship we would have drowned. And there are many such cases. And the verse is full of it (Psalms 107:23): ״They that go down to the sea in ships, that do business in great waters, these saw the works of HaShem…״ and so forth." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: ״the great sea-monsters״ — this is Leviathan and its mate. And it said ״the great״ on account of the extraordinary mass of their bodies, for its body is placed in the Ocean sea that surrounds the globe of the earth — like the expression ״the great luminaries״, which are many times larger than the globe of the earth. Perhaps it is for this reason that it did not say concerning them ״ויהי כן״ — for their permanent existence did not stand in both of them, since our Sages of blessed memory said in the chapter המוכר את הבית: Leviathan, male and female, He created them; and were they to mate with one another they would destroy the entire world. What did the Holy One, blessed be He, do? He castrated the male and killed the female and salted her for the righteous in the time to come, as it is said (Isaiah 27:1): ״And He will slay the serpent that is in the sea״. And this verse proves concerning Leviathan that there were two, which is what it says (Isaiah, ibid.): ״upon Leviathan the fleeing serpent, and upon Leviathan the crooked serpent״ — and what it says, ״And He will slay the serpent״, indicates that one remained alone without its mate. And so David said (Psalms 104:26): ״Leviathan, whom You have formed to sport therein״, that is, that it plays in the Ocean sea. And the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Job (Job 40:25): ״Can you draw out Leviathan with a hook?״ And what it says, ״the sea-monsters״ (plural) — to include the other fish of the sea, which are alluded to by the pluralizing ״את״. And perhaps they are food for Leviathan." "<b>And the great master, my teacher</b> R. Solomon, may his light shine, explained the sense of this Midrash: that one should not dismiss the possibility that for the righteous in the World to Come there will be an actual feast according to the plain sense of the words as they came down to our masters in the Midrashim and the Aggadot. As our Sages of blessed memory expounded — R. Berekhiah in the name of R. Yitzḥak: ״Ariston״ — a great feast the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to make for the righteous in the time to come; ״ariston״ means a feast. And whoever did not eat nevelot and trefot in this world merits to eat of it in the World to Come, as it is written (Leviticus 7:24): ״But the fat of carrion and the fat of a torn thing…״ and so forth. Also in the chapter המוכר (page 75a) we find: the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to make a feast for the righteous from the flesh of Leviathan — it did not say ״from Leviathan״ but ״from its flesh״. And the ultimate intent of that feast is not the intent of eating and drinking alone, merely to fill the belly and for the pleasure of the body and the throat — for that is not the matter of the World to Come. Rather, it is already well known that through the agency of eating and drinking, the powers of the body are aroused, and when the bodily powers are roused, the powers of the soul are likewise aroused and strengthened, toward whatever matter the eater intends to draw them and direct them toward — for eating and drinking are great causes for gladness of heart and for distancing sadness and worry. And it is known that when joy increases, the rational power within the soul is strengthened and becomes more prepared to apprehend intelligibles — as in what is said of Elisha (2 Kings 3): ״Take me a minstrel״, and our Sages of blessed memory expounded: the Shekhinah does not rest neither from amidst laziness nor from amidst sadness, but only from amidst a matter of joy. And like the matter of the delicacies that Isaac requested — and the sense was explained: ״in order that my soul may bless you״ — one opinion reads ״in order that I may bless you״ — like the matter of Aaron's feast and all the elders of Israel with Moses' father-in-law, before Elohim; and like the matter of Samuel's feast, concerning which it is written, ״the sons of the prophets were standing and prophesying before him״. And so it is possible that those feasts for the righteous with flesh and fish were created during the six days of Creation for this purpose, with a degree of delight that this world is not fit to attain, except in the World to Come. And those who are invited to those feasts — that is the ultimate desideratum for the human species. And perhaps those foods, being very pure in nature, were prepared from the beginning of Creation to multiply the intellect in nature, in the manner of what is written: ״and the tree was desirable to make wise״. Also, it is well known today among certain foods and simples that they are by their nature beneficial for this; how much more so those that were created during the six days of Creation, perhaps for this intent. And like what happened with the manna, which is a bodily food, given during the era of the Torah to a people that was prepared to know truths and contemplate the intelligibles, as separated intellects — and it contained no waste at all that would disturb the intellect. And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said, that it was entirely absorbed in the organs. And our Sages of blessed memory expounded (Psalms 78:25): ״the bread of the mighty did man eat״ — bread that is absorbed in the organs. And our Sages of blessed memory already likened it to the sustenance of the righteous in the time to come, saying in Ḥagigah: the Holy One, blessed be He, grinds manna in the firmament for the righteous. And perhaps this feast prepared for the righteous will take place at the end of the era in which the order of bodily activities — eating and drinking — still applies, and after it the practice of that order will be abolished, and they will sit with their crowns upon their heads and be refreshed by the radiance of the Shekhinah. And that feast will be for them like the matter of the festival assembly, except that this one is the consummation of sacred time and that one is the consummation of profane time." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): the matter of Leviathan, male and female He created them, alludes to the matter of the intellect and the soul. And the Sages of Truth explained why man was not compelled in his actions — so that he might be likened to the separated intellects, persisting constantly in the intention for which he was created, which is the service of HaShem and knowledge of His truth, which is the activity of the intellect. Know that the human soul is wisdom in potential, prepared to receive the intellect through the rational faculty within it. And when it is drawn after the intellect and cleaves to it, the intellect displays its activities upon it, and the soul is as matter and the intellect is its form. Therefore the intellect is called male and the soul female. And לויתן (leviathan) signifies joining, from the language of ״ילוה אישי אלי״ (Genesis 29:34); it also signifies splendor, from the language of לוית חן — that which is joined to the soul. And when the soul is drawn after it, it is for her לוית חן, and it is called לויתן. And when she cleaves to it and is joined to it, she returns to be like it — they call it לויתן male and female, as though they are one species and one matter, except that this one is the active male and this one is the receptive female. And therefore they said לויתן זכר ונקבה. And sometimes they call them לויתן because they join and cleave in the body. Now, man — after he was brought into being upon this intention that we have stated, why was he not created straightaway likened to the separated intellects, and why should the human soul not have an aroused power from which it sometimes desires a thing and sometimes despises it, in order to flee from it? — so that his soul and his intellect would cleave to each other constantly and be like the separated intellects. And this is what they said: ״Were they to mate with each other they would destroy the entire world״ — because the power of generation would be abolished from the human species, for the matter of generation is not from the activities of the intellect but from the activities of the desiring soul. And likewise, were the matter of the intellect to persist constantly upon the soul and cleave to it always, the soul would be bonded on high without separation and would be sustained by the power of the body, in the manner of Elijah. What did He do? He castrated the male and killed the female. Castration is the removal of the generative power; and killing is the removal of life. Therefore they called the gathering of the thing and its removal ״castration and killing״. And ״He salted her for the righteous in the time to come״ — the thing that causes permanence they called by the term salting, as they said: salt is money that is diminished, and from it, ״a covenant of salt forever״ — because salt preserves them from receiving loss. And the intention in all this is that castration is the destruction of the organ of generation and the removal of its power, and it is the cause of the male's separation from the female and the cause of loss in living creatures. And therefore He said ״and He salted her״, alluding that the soul of the righteous has permanence and endures in the World to Come. And since the soul and the intellect are taken from the sea of wisdom, and they are hidden things, they drew the metaphor and likened them to fish that were created from the sea. Thus far the words of the great master, my teacher, may his light shine." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: ״And Elohim created the great sea-monsters״ — you already know the statement of our Sages of blessed memory that the angels were created on the fifth day. And ״the great sea-monsters״ are the four camps of the Shekhinah, which are the choir for His Shekhinah, to praise and exalt the Holy One, blessed be He, with songs and praises, from the language of (Judges 5:11) ״שם יתנו צדקות ה׳״. And it already mentioned ״and let fowl fly״ — the intent being concerning the angels, of whom it is written ״ובשתים יעופף״. And therefore it is called עוף, whose wings are doubled to two, and it flies with two; and the Targum is ״וכפלת ותעוף״. From this you learn that the name עוף is applied to all that flies with two, whether bodily or spiritual. And it says ״upon the earth״ — upon the place that is the footstool of His feet." "<b>And you should reflect</b> on how He added an explanation and said, 'upon the earth, upon the face of the firmament of the heavens' — as though He said that what He calls 'upon the earth' means 'upon the face of the firmament of the heavens.' This is like the language of (Ezekiel 1:15) ״והנה אופן אחד בארץ״ — 'and behold one ophan upon the earth' — which means upon the surface of the sphere's base; and so Targum Yonatan rendered: מלרע לרום שמיא — 'from below toward the height of heaven.'" "<b>And in the Midrash</b> of Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai I saw: 'And fowl' — this is Michael, as it is said (Isaiah 6:6) ״ויעף אלי״ — 'and it flew to me.' ״יעופף״ — this is Gabriel, as it is said (Daniel 9:21) ״מועף ביעף״ — 'swiftly in flight.' 'Upon the earth' — this is Raphael. 'Upon the face of the firmament of the heavens' — this is Uriel.' End quote. And this is the secret of (Song 3:10) ״מרכבו ארגמן״ — 'his chariot of purple' — and the discerning will understand." "<b>And every living creature that moves.</b> This is the four-faced creature that Ezekiel referred to regarding it (Ezekiel 10:20) ״היא החיה אשר ראיתי תחת אלהי ישראל בנהר כבר״ — 'this is the creature that I saw beneath the God of Israel at the river Chebar.' And from this he said ״אשר שרצו המים״ — 'which the waters swarmed' — just as he said 'at the river Chebar.'" "<b>And every winged fowl after its kind.</b> He explained that the angels created on the fifth day are distinct species, rank after rank, one above the other. Some of them are from water, and some of them are from fire; and there is fire that repels fire, and there is consuming fire — and understand this."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :register {:flags {7 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:27:09.656332828Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}